  "Number": 25,
  "Question": "One of the factors of \\(x^4+2x^2+9\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x^2+3\\)",
    "B": "\\(x+1\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^2-3\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2-2x-3\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "A polynomial factor must divide the given polynomial with no remainder. Let\n\\[\nP(x)=x^4+2x^2+9.\n\\]\nFor the quadratic candidates in A and C, direct multiplication gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x)&=(x^2+3)(x^2-1)+12,\\\\\nP(x)&=(x^2-3)(x^2+5)+24.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe nonzero constant remainders rule out both \\(x^2+3\\) and \\(x^2-3\\).\n\nFor the remaining candidates, notice that both \\(x+1\\) and \\(x^2-2x-3\\) equal zero when \\(x=-1\\). If either were a factor of \\(P\\), substituting \\(-1\\) into the product would give \\(P(-1)=0\\). Instead,\n\\[\nP(-1)=1+2+9=12.\n\\]\nThus B and D are also ruled out. None of the four listed polynomials is a factor, so the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
